{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791346370987,"sessionID":"ses_eeb753877ffeEZ5M2IaWa5Y4aK","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"PoFLFjpibUWSvY29","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/user/auto-psych/docs/auto_rsa/DATASETS.md"},"output":"<path>/home/user/auto-psych/docs/auto_rsa/DATASETS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Candidate datasets beyond pragmods (survey, 2026-10-06)\n2: \n3: ## Status (2026-10-07): ingested\n4: \n5: Built by `src/rsa/ingest/` (`uv run python -m src.rsa.ingest.run --sources ...`;\n6: combine with pragmods via `src.rsa.ingest.combine`). Details, exclusions and\n7: gaps: `src/pipelines/outer_loop/projects/rsa_reference/data/README.md`.\n8: \n9: | source | data | licence | in repo | participants (included) | paper's N reproduced |\n10: |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n11: | `mayn_demberg_2026` (PLoS One) | OSF 5ab3f | CC-BY 4.0 | CSV + pin | 306 (254) | yes: 300/8/292/23/15/254 |\n12: | `mayn_demberg_2023` (Open Mind) | GitHub refgame_stimuli_methods @ d7d4aec | none | pin only (CSV in `data/rsa/external/`) | 237 (57/55/56/60) | yes (Table 1) |\n13: | `mayn_demberg_2022` (CogSci) | GitHub refgame_cogsci22 @ b3a2b5e | none | pin only | 115 (47 pilot + 68 main) | main 68 (cited in the 2026 paper); paper not retrievable |\n14: | `sikos_2021` (PLoS One) | supporting files S1-S3 | CC-BY 4.0 | CSV + pin | 7,488 (5,625) | yes, every N and exclusion count |\n15: \n16: Not ingested: **Duff, Mayn & Demberg 2026** (OSF 7uwx9/ad685): participants got\n17: feedback after every reference-game trial and a speaker pre-training, so the\n18: choices are learned under reinforcement. Excluded by the PI: Franke & Degen\n19: 2016, child data, imagined-child / ChatGPT-speaker conditions (f5nmv,\n20: perceptions_of_chatgpt), slider studies (erbn3), the listener-adaptation study\n21: (5d2f6), Franke, Tsvilodub & Carcassi 2024.\n22: \n23: The survey below is unchanged from 2026-10-06.\n24: \n25: Survey of public reference-game data that could join the seed data. Sources:\n26: problang-v2's and the pragmods paper's bibliographies, plus web search.\n27: Fit score: **A** = maps to our trial schema (objects x binary features,\n28: word heard, forced choice) with modest work; **B** = needs a schema\n29: extension; **C** = not usable.\n30: \n31: Files checked in a cloud session: GitHub-hosted data only. PLoS, OSF,\n32: Zenodo and escholarship are blocked there, so those sets are unverified.\n33: \n34: ## A: maps to our schema\n35: \n36: | Dataset | Paradigm | Size | Where | Verified |\n37: |---|---|---|---|---|\n38: | **Franke & Degen 2016**, PLoS One | Simple/complex implicature + fillers; 3 objects on 2 feature dimensions, some features not nameable; fixed set of 4 messages | ~60/experiment, **66 listener trials per person**; comprehension + production | PLoS supporting files (S1/S2 Data CSV): https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0154854 | no |\n39: | **Mayn & Demberg series** (CogSci 2022; Open Mind 2023, 2025; PLoS One 2026; Duff et al. 2026) | Franke & Degen game on Prolific, with individual differences (reasoning, ToM, working memory); 2025 manipulates speaker identity (adult vs 4-year-old) | ~60+ per study, many trials each, with covariates | OSF: https://osf.io/5ab3f/ (2026 PLoS), https://osf.io/7uwx9/ (Duff 2026), https://osf.io/w7yqg?view_only=fa7f9034042946f0928d2c772e0a23ad (2025), prereg https://osf.io/dh8wm (2023; data link in the article) | no |\n40: | **Sikos, Venhuizen, Drenhaus & Crocker 2021**, PLoS One | Close Frank & Goodman 2012 replication over more context types; listener, speaker, salience conditions | trial-level in supporting files | https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0248388 | no |\n41: | **Yoon & Frank 2019**, JECP (`ejyoon/simpimp_rs`) | Children 2-5, tablet; 2 objects as sets of items, one-noun utterance | 2,792 trials, 180 children, 8-16 trials each | GitHub (no licence file; Zenodo 1285235) | **yes** |\n42: | **Qing & Franke 2015** | Frank & Goodman replication, colour/shape | aggregate counts only (listener 2x3, production 3x4, salience prior) | problang app-04 | yes (counts) |\n43: \n44: Mapping notes:\n45: - **Franke & Degen and Mayn & Demberg:** a feature dimension's value becomes\n46:   a 0/1 column. We would need to mark which columns have a word (some\n47:   features cannot be named), a small schema addition. Theirs and the pragmods\n48:   sequences are the only sets with many trials per person, which\n49:   individual-level models need.\n50: - **Yoon & Frank:** the display has to be rebuilt from `trial_type` x\n51:   `item_num`, and the 2vs2 reading should be checked against `experiment/`\n52:   stimuli. `sample2.csv` has positions and words; `sample1.csv` does not. The\n53:   final file is already filtered (`log.csv` has the decisions). These are\n54:   children, so treat them as a separate population.\n55: \n56: ## B: needs a schema extension\n57: - **Degen et al. 2020** (`thegricean/overinformativeness`): production of\n58:   redundant size/colour; free text coded to mentions. The final data are\n59:   unconfirmed in the repo.\n60: - **Degen & Franke 2012; Degen, Franke & Jäger 2013:** costly artificial\n61:   messages. Data not located.\n62: - **Kreiss & Degen 2020; Waldon & Degen 2021:** colour-adjective production.\n63:   Data not located.\n64: - **Monroe et al. 2017** (colors in context, GitHub): continuous HSL colours\n65:   and free text, 57,946 messages.\n66: - **Bohn et al. 2021/22** (`manuelbohn/mcc`): word learning with combined\n67:   cues. The display is not stored per trial.\n68: \n69: ## C: not usable here\n70: - Tangrams/convention formation (Hawkins et al.).\n71: - Scalar-implicature star ratings (Peloquin & Frank). `rrrsa`'s pragmods data\n72:   are aggregated pragmods.\n73: - Politeness; embedded scalars.\n74: \n75: ## Duplicates\n76: - **Vogel et al. 2014** is pragmods E8 (levels), already in the seed.\n77: \n78: ## To fetch or allowlist\n79: - **Allowlist** `osf.io`, `api.osf.io`, `files.osf.io` and `journals.plos.org`\n80:   in the cloud environment, or download into\n81:   `src/pipelines/outer_loop/projects/rsa_reference/references/data/` from a\n82:   local session.\n83: - **Franke, Tsvilodub & Carcassi 2024** (https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09012)\n84:   names an OSF repository of human reference-game data collected to test LLMs.\n85: - **Ask the authors:** Stiller, Goodman & Frank 2015; Fortier et al. 2023\n86:   (Shipibo-Konibo children); trial-level Qing & Franke 2015; raw Frank &\n87:   Goodman 2012 bets.\n\n(End of file - total 87 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Candidate datasets beyond pragmods (survey, 2026-10-06)\n\n## Status (2026-10-07): ingested\n\nBuilt by `src/rsa/ingest/` (`uv run python -m src.rsa.ingest.run --sources ...`;\ncombine with pragmods via `src.rsa.ingest.combine`). Details, exclusions and\ngaps: `src/pipelines/outer_loop/projects/rsa_reference/data/README.md`.\n\n| source | data | licence | in repo | participants (included) | paper's N reproduced |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `mayn_demberg_2026` (PLoS One) | OSF 5ab3f | CC-BY 4.0 | CSV + pin | 306 (254) | yes: 300/8/292/23/15/254 |\n| `mayn_demberg_2023` (Open Mind) | GitHub refgame_stimuli_methods @ d7d4aec | none | pin only (CSV in `data/rsa/external/`) | 237 (57/55/56/60) | yes (Table 1) |\n| `mayn_demberg_2022` (CogSci) | GitHub refgame_cogsci22 @ b3a2b5e | none | pin only | 115 (47 pilot + 68 main) | main 68 (cited in the 2026 paper); paper not retrievable |\n| `sikos_2021` (PLoS One) | supporting files S1-S3 | CC-BY 4.0 | CSV + pin | 7,488 (5,625) | yes, every N and exclusion count |\n\nNot ingested: **Duff, Mayn & Demberg 2026** (OSF 7uwx9/ad685): participants got\nfeedback after every reference-game trial and a speaker pre-training, so the\nchoices are learned under reinforcement. Excluded by the PI: Franke & Degen\n2016, child data, imagined-child / ChatGPT-speaker conditions (f5nmv,\nperceptions_of_chatgpt), slider studies (erbn3), the listener-adaptation study","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/user/auto-psych/docs/auto_rsa/DATASETS.md","text":"# Candidate datasets beyond pragmods (survey, 2026-10-06)\n\n## Status (2026-10-07): ingested\n\nBuilt by `src/rsa/ingest/` (`uv run python -m src.rsa.ingest.run --sources ...`;\ncombine with pragmods via `src.rsa.ingest.combine`). Details, exclusions and\ngaps: `src/pipelines/outer_loop/projects/rsa_reference/data/README.md`.\n\n| source | data | licence | in repo | participants (included) | paper's N reproduced |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `mayn_demberg_2026` (PLoS One) | OSF 5ab3f | CC-BY 4.0 | CSV + pin | 306 (254) | yes: 300/8/292/23/15/254 |\n| `mayn_demberg_2023` (Open Mind) | GitHub refgame_stimuli_methods @ d7d4aec | none | pin only (CSV in `data/rsa/external/`) | 237 (57/55/56/60) | yes (Table 1) |\n| `mayn_demberg_2022` (CogSci) | GitHub refgame_cogsci22 @ b3a2b5e | none | pin only | 115 (47 pilot + 68 main) | main 68 (cited in the 2026 paper); paper not retrievable |\n| `sikos_2021` (PLoS One) | supporting files S1-S3 | CC-BY 4.0 | CSV + pin | 7,488 (5,625) | yes, every N and exclusion count |\n\nNot ingested: **Duff, Mayn & Demberg 2026** (OSF 7uwx9/ad685): participants got\nfeedback after every reference-game trial and a speaker pre-training, so the\nchoices are learned under reinforcement. Excluded by the PI: Franke & Degen\n2016, child data, imagined-child / ChatGPT-speaker conditions (f5nmv,\nperceptions_of_chatgpt), slider studies (erbn3), the listener-adaptation study\n(5d2f6), Franke, Tsvilodub & Carcassi 2024.\n\nThe survey below is unchanged from 2026-10-06.\n\nSurvey of public reference-game data that could join the seed data. Sources:\nproblang-v2's and the pragmods paper's bibliographies, plus web search.\nFit score: **A** = maps to our trial schema (objects x binary features,\nword heard, forced choice) with modest work; **B** = needs a schema\nextension; **C** = not usable.\n\nFiles checked in a cloud session: GitHub-hosted data only. PLoS, OSF,\nZenodo and escholarship are blocked there, so those sets are unverified.\n\n## A: maps to our schema\n\n| Dataset | Paradigm | Size | Where | Verified |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Franke & Degen 2016**, PLoS One | Simple/complex implicature + fillers; 3 objects on 2 feature dimensions, some features not nameable; fixed set of 4 messages | ~60/experiment, **66 listener trials per person**; comprehension + production | PLoS supporting files (S1/S2 Data CSV): https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0154854 | no |\n| **Mayn & Demberg series** (CogSci 2022; Open Mind 2023, 2025; PLoS One 2026; Duff et al. 2026) | Franke & Degen game on Prolific, with individual differences (reasoning, ToM, working memory); 2025 manipulates speaker identity (adult vs 4-year-old) | ~60+ per study, many trials each, with covariates | OSF: https://osf.io/5ab3f/ (2026 PLoS), https://osf.io/7uwx9/ (Duff 2026), https://osf.io/w7yqg?view_only=fa7f9034042946f0928d2c772e0a23ad (2025), prereg https://osf.io/dh8wm (2023; data link in the article) | no |\n| **Sikos, Venhuizen, Drenhaus & Crocker 2021**, PLoS One | Close Frank & Goodman 2012 replication over more context types; listener, speaker, salience conditions | trial-level in supporting files | https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0248388 | no |\n| **Yoon & Frank 2019**, JECP (`ejyoon/simpimp_rs`) | Children 2-5, tablet; 2 objects as sets of items, one-noun utterance | 2,792 trials, 180 children, 8-16 trials each | GitHub (no licence file; Zenodo 1285235) | **yes** |\n| **Qing & Franke 2015** | Frank & Goodman replication, colour/shape | aggregate counts only (listener 2x3, production 3x4, salience prior) | problang app-04 | yes (counts) |\n\nMapping notes:\n- **Franke & Degen and Mayn & Demberg:** a feature dimension's value becomes\n  a 0/1 column. We would need to mark which columns have a word (some\n  features cannot be named), a small schema addition. Theirs and the pragmods\n  sequences are the only sets with many trials per person, which\n  individual-level models need.\n- **Yoon & Frank:** the display has to be rebuilt from `trial_type` x\n  `item_num`, and the 2vs2 reading should be checked against `experiment/`\n  stimuli. `sample2.csv` has positions and words; `sample1.csv` does not. The\n  final file is already filtered (`log.csv` has the decisions). These are\n  children, so treat them as a separate population.\n\n## B: needs a schema extension\n- **Degen et al. 2020** (`thegricean/overinformativeness`): production of\n  redundant size/colour; free text coded to mentions. The final data are\n  unconfirmed in the repo.\n- **Degen & Franke 2012; Degen, Franke & Jäger 2013:** costly artificial\n  messages. Data not located.\n- **Kreiss & Degen 2020; Waldon & Degen 2021:** colour-adjective production.\n  Data not located.\n- **Monroe et al. 2017** (colors in context, GitHub): continuous HSL colours\n  and free text, 57,946 messages.\n- **Bohn et al. 2021/22** (`manuelbohn/mcc`): word learning with combined\n  cues. The display is not stored per trial.\n\n## C: not usable here\n- Tangrams/convention formation (Hawkins et al.).\n- Scalar-implicature star ratings (Peloquin & Frank). `rrrsa`'s pragmods data\n  are aggregated pragmods.\n- Politeness; embedded scalars.\n\n## Duplicates\n- **Vogel et al. 2014** is pragmods E8 (levels), already in the seed.\n\n## To fetch or allowlist\n- **Allowlist** `osf.io`, `api.osf.io`, `files.osf.io` and `journals.plos.org`\n  in the cloud environment, or download into\n  `src/pipelines/outer_loop/projects/rsa_reference/references/data/` from a\n  local session.\n- **Franke, Tsvilodub & Carcassi 2024** (https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09012)\n  names an OSF repository of human reference-game data collected to test LLMs.\n- **Ask the authors:** Stiller, Goodman & Frank 2015; Fortier et al. 2023\n  (Shipibo-Konibo children); trial-level Qing & Franke 2015; raw Frank &\n  Goodman 2012 bets.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":87,"totalLines":87,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/auto_rsa/DATASETS.md","time":{"start":1791346370952,"end":1791346370981}},"metadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":"EtgBCtUBAWkUfRPMIVoGeSvD2/ceD6/klZuLs4OIEaD3R76nhuiWgiCxrO9jX186l2xntIssUMqpSZtRfOM2qw/VwV3vjy4iC5KKA+sDnbsmrWd69KZ2Nw4UwepMUGmxg2LoBrbNbCBf1imvY29Dc4grmfbeEWc5kflWcEaPcagDJ9qauvB230JVSnxoo9hqXkhs0F2Q93u4umpAYls7W0nX46EzzaavYl+5SFUTJrlFCr1dbAbroju06HT1SNJ9Q4nNQX1ylJcJO8VW4GhN9zux+KbiROjsUzgy"}},"id":"prt_114906980001JYFrD3ij50Z1rB","sessionID":"ses_eeb753877ffeEZ5M2IaWa5Y4aK","messageID":"msg_114905f42001FVTxE0WOSiCNb7"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791346371253,"sessionID":"ses_eeb753877ffeEZ5M2IaWa5Y4aK","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"XUC334XWdZvc6MAX","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/user/auto-psych/docs/auto_rsa/SMOKE_RESULTS.md"},"output":"<path>/home/user/auto-psych/docs/auto_rsa/SMOKE_RESULTS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # RSA loop smoke test with real Gemini agents: results\n2: \n3: Run 2026-10-06 23:14 to 2026-10-07 00:11 UTC (56 min), in a disposable\n4: claude.ai cloud container (4 CPUs), on branch `auto-rsa-smoke` (from\n5: `auto-rsa` at `c1dd78f`), following `HANDOFF_smoke_test.md`:\n6: \n7: ```bash\n8: uv run python -m src.rsa.loop.run --results data/rsa/loop_smoke \\\n9:     --max-iterations 1 --candidate-count 3 --no-sandbox \\\n10:     --num-warmup 500 --num-samples 500 --num-chains 2\n11: ```\n12: \n13: Agents: opencode 1.18.35, `google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview` (the default).\n14: Data: all 6,703 included forced-choice pragmods trials. Run outputs that are\n15: small are committed under `data/rsa/loop_smoke/` (report, history, ledger,\n16: each candidate's files and agent logs; not `.fit_cache/`, `responses.csv`\n17: or opencode's `.xdg_data/` snapshots).\n18: \n19: ## Verdict\n20: \n21: **Success on the handoff's criterion. The self-check did not work in this run,\n22: and the repair path was never exercised.**\n23: \n24: - Two of three slots wrote a memo model that passed every gate (code, contract,\n25:   fit, convergence, finite ELPD, novelty) on the **first** attempt. One of\n26:   them, `rsa_l2_salience`, is the new best model: −4955.5, which is\n27:   17.7 ± 2.7 nats ahead of the previous best seed `rsa_l1_salience`.\n28: - Neither agent ever saw a self-check result. Every self-check call failed: on\n29:   a CLI bug (now fixed) or on opencode's 120 s shell timeout (fixed in the\n30:   brief and primer). Their models passed because they stayed close to the\n31:   seed files' memo, not because the self-check caught anything.\n32: - One slot (refine incumbent) wrote no `candidate.py`, twice. No candidate was\n33:   rejected, so **no repair ran**. Whether the rejection reasons let a repair\n34:   succeed is still untested.\n35: - The agents hit **no memo errors at all**. Each one read seed files first and\n36:   changed them in small steps (a new prior, an extra level). The primer's\n37:   gotchas were not tested in anger.\n38: \n39: ## Per slot\n40: \n41: | Slot | Role | Outcome | Model | ELPD-LOO | Novelty: nearest (RMSE) | Self-check | Agent wall time | Tokens (in / cached / out / reasoning) | Cost |\n42: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n43: | 1 | explore (lens: \"a genuinely different family\") | **admitted** | `literal_speaker_inverter` | −5046.1 (90.6 ± 15.3 behind) | `rsa_l1_shared_prior` (0.045) | 3 calls: usage error, then killed at 120 s twice | 12 min | 77k / 63k / 0.8k / 15.1k | $0.36 |\n44: | 2 | refine incumbent (`rsa_l1_salience`) | **no file** | (drafts in `/tmp`, never in its dir) | — | — | 1 call, killed at 120 s | 20 min (killed at the limit) | 57k / 152k / 0.9k / 2.0k | $0.18 |\n45: | 2 retry | same | **no file** | — | — | — | none | 20 min (killed at the limit) | not reported (killed) | ? |\n46: | 3 | refine chosen | **admitted** | `rsa_l2_salience` | **−4955.5 (best)** | `rsa_l1_salience` (0.010) | 3 calls: usage error, then killed at 120 s twice | 19 min | 39k / 256k / 1.1k / 3.0k | $0.18 |\n47: \n48: Reported spend: **$0.71** for the round. The killed retry reported no usage,\n49: so the true figure is a little higher. Token use per slot is small: ~33k of\n50: each prompt is the brief, primer and handbook, which caching mostly absorbs.\n51: \n52: Phase timing: seed fits 6.5 min (5 models); agents 20 min (bound by the\n53: agent timeout of slot 2); the retry 20 min (killed again); admission fits of\n54: the two candidates 4 min; scoring and report 4 min.\n55: \n56: ### Slot 1: `literal_speaker_inverter` (explore)\n57: \n58: > Listeners invert a zero-order \"literal\" speaker (S0) who simply picks one\n59: > of the object's features uniformly at random, without simulating a\n60: > listener. The pragmatic preference for simpler objects emerges entirely\n61: > from probability dilution.\n62: \n63: This is a genuinely different mechanism: L1 over a literal speaker, with no\n64: informativity term, and a prior on familiarization, grayscale and feature\n65: count. It is 90.6 nats behind the best model, yet clearly better than the\n66: literal listener. It is a useful contrast: the data favour a speaker who\n67: weighs informativity. The agent spent its first step on 15k reasoning tokens\n68: and wrote all three files in one heredoc, and its memo compiled first time.\n69: \n70: ### Slot 2: refine incumbent, no candidate (twice)\n71: \n72: The first agent read the seeds and drafted a depth-2 salience model in\n73: `/tmp/cand_l2/` and in `test_l2_salience.py` **in the repository root**. It ran\n74: the self-check on `/tmp/cand_l2`, which was killed at 120 s. It then pivoted to\n75: an L1 + grayscale-prior variant (`/tmp/cand_l1_color/`, `test_l1_color.py` in\n76: the repo root), and was killed at the 1,200 s agent limit while that check\n77: ran. It never wrote to its candidate directory.\n78: \n79: The retry agent's first event came 13 min 46 s after spawn (one `cat` of a\n80: seed file); it then went silent until the 20 min limit killed it. This looks\n81: like Gemini preview latency or a stalled request, not anything in the brief.\n82: \n83: Under bubblewrap (production) the stray files in the repo root would have\n84: failed (read-only tree), and so would the `/tmp` drafts once the sandbox was\n85: gone. Either way the slot would be empty.\n86: \n87: ### Slot 3: `rsa_l2_salience` (refine chosen)\n88: \n89: > I refine the `rsa_l2` model by giving its top-level pragmatic listener the\n90: > learned salience prior over objects (based on feature count and\n91: > familiarization) …\n92: \n93: This is a single stated change, the refinement brief's intended use: the\n94: salience prior of `rsa_l1_salience` grafted onto the top level of `rsa_l2`.\n95: It is the round's discovery. The best model now has a depth-2 listener *and*\n96: a salience prior, and leads by 17.7 nats (6.5 SE). Its novelty margin is small\n97: but clear: RMSE 0.010 from `rsa_l1_salience`, against a threshold of 0.002.\n98: Slot 2's abandoned draft was nearly the same model (the prior at every level),\n99: so the two refinement slots converged on one idea.\n100: \n101: ## Bugs found and fixed (each with a test)\n102: \n103: 1. **The documented self-check command did not parse.**\n104:    - **Cause:** The brief and primer give\n105:      `check_candidate <dir> --responses <csv>`, but the tyro CLI declared\n106:      `candidate_dir` as a required `--candidate-dir` flag.\n107:    - **Effect:** All three agents ran the documented form first and got a\n108:      usage error.\n109:    - **Fix:** `candidate_dir` is now positional\n110:      (`tyro.conf.Positional`, `src/rsa/loop/check_candidate.py`).\n111:    - **Test:** `tests/test_rsa_loop_brief.py::test_the_documented_self_check_command_parses`\n112:      parses the command exactly as `CHECK_COMMAND` writes it.\n113: 2. **The self-check never finished inside an agent.**\n114:    - **Cause:** opencode's shell tool kills a command after 120 s by default.\n115:      The self-check takes 2 min 11 s on an idle 4-CPU container, and longer\n116:      while three agents run checks at once.\n117:    - **Effect:** All five correctly formed self-check calls were killed\n118:      before printing a verdict.\n119:    - **Verified:** opencode's shell tool accepts a `timeout` parameter, and a\n120:      150 s command with `timeout: 300000` completed.\n121:    - **Fix:** `CONTEXT.md` (`brief.CHECK_SHELL_TIMEOUT_MS` = 900,000 ms) and the\n122:      primer's self-check section now say to pass that timeout, to run the\n123:      check once per change, and to write and test files **only in the\n124:      candidate directory** (slot 2's failure).\n125:    - **Test:** asserted in `test_each_role_gets_its_documents_and_the_handbook`.\n126: 3. **No `token_usage.jsonl`.**\n127:    - **Cause:** The handoff says the launcher writes it, but\n128:      `src/rsa/loop/run.py` never started the usage log.\n129:    - **Effect:** This run's spend above is read from the agents' own logs.\n130:    - **Fix:** The CLI now starts the usage log at\n131:      `<results>/token_usage.jsonl` and writes `token_usage_summary.json` in a\n132:      `finally`, as the PyMC loop does.\n133:    - **Test:** `tests/test_rsa_loop_run.py`.\n134: \n135: ## Other findings and recommendations\n136: \n137: **Recommendations, in priority order:**\n138: \n139: 1. **Raise `--agent-timeout-sec` from 1,200 to ~2,400 for production.** With\n140:    a working self-check, a careful agent now runs 1 to 3 checks of 2 to 10 min\n141:    each. 20 min would end many agents mid-check. Slot 3 already used 19 min,\n142:    mostly waiting on killed checks.\n143: 2. **Turn off opencode's snapshots for loop agents.** opencode keeps a git\n144:    snapshot of its working directory in each agent's private `XDG_DATA_HOME`\n145:    (`<candidate>/.xdg_data/opencode/snapshot`). With the agents running from\n146:    the repo root, that was ~1 GB per agent: **3.9 GB for this four-agent\n147:    round**. At 5 rounds × 6 slots plus retries and repairs on Sherlock, that\n148:    is 30 to 60 GB per run. The scrubbed agent tree will be smaller, but not\n149:    free. opencode's config has `\"snapshot\": false`. Set it in the launcher's\n150:    per-agent opencode config (`src/runtime/coding_agent.py`, shared with the\n151:    PyMC loop, so not changed here), or delete `.xdg_data/opencode/snapshot`\n152:    when the agent ends.\n153: 3. **Pruning at 2 × clustered dse prunes nothing on pragmods.** The end-of-run\n154:    prune kept all 7 models, even `literal_listener` at 572 nats behind. The\n155:    stimulus-clustered SE over the 59 display clusters is large: 331.6 for\n156:    `literal_listener`, a ratio of 1.73. Every model's ratio to the best was\n157:    between 1.2 and 1.7. A few large, heterogeneous clusters (displays shared\n158:    across experiments) dominate the variance. Pruning works as written, but on\n159:    this data it never fires, so the live set will hit the cap of 8 by round 2\n160:    and the cap will do all the selection. Consider clustering by display\n161:    *within* experiment, a lower multiplier, or a per-cluster sign test.\n162:    Decide before the production run; it is a design choice, not a bug.\n163: 4. **Re-run the smoke test** with the fixes, before Sherlock. It should take\n164:    ~1 h; consider `--candidate-count 3 --max-iterations 2` so that round 2\n165:    sees a live set with agent-written models. Look for: (a) agents now\n166:    reading a PASS/FAIL line; (b) at least one rejection, so the repair path\n167:    runs. If none comes naturally, a deliberately strict novelty threshold for\n168:    one round would force rejections.\n169: 5. **Gemini latency.** The retry agent took 13 min 46 s to make its first tool\n170:    call. If that recurs, try a Flash model for retries (`--agent-model`), or\n171:    accept the cost of an empty slot.\n172: 6. **The two refinement slots converged** on \"L2 + salience\". With 6 slots\n173:    (two refining the incumbent) that will happen more. The PyMC loop has the\n174:    same structure, so watch it in production rather than change it now.\n175: \n176: **No new memo gotchas.** No agent hit a memo compile or runtime error, so the\n177: primer's list in `rsa_theory.md` and PLAN.md stands unchanged. The agents\n178: worked around the language by copying the seeds' memo verbatim and changing\n179: `choice_probs` and the prior. That is safe, but it may keep exploration close\n180: to the seeds. An exploratory slot that needs new memo structure (a lexical\n181: uncertainty model, a QUD speaker) is still untested.\n182: \n183: **Hypothesis style.** Slot 3's `hypothesis.md` reads as a change log (\"I refine\n184: `rsa_l2` by …\") rather than a claim a psychologist could test. The refinement\n185: menu asks the agent to name the model it refines, so this follows the brief.\n186: A sentence in the refinement brief could ask for the claim first and the\n187: lineage second.\n\n(End of file - total 187 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# RSA loop smoke test with real Gemini agents: results\n\nRun 2026-10-06 23:14 to 2026-10-07 00:11 UTC (56 min), in a disposable\nclaude.ai cloud container (4 CPUs), on branch `auto-rsa-smoke` (from\n`auto-rsa` at `c1dd78f`), following `HANDOFF_smoke_test.md`:\n\n```bash\nuv run python -m src.rsa.loop.run --results data/rsa/loop_smoke \\\n    --max-iterations 1 --candidate-count 3 --no-sandbox \\\n    --num-warmup 500 --num-samples 500 --num-chains 2\n```\n\nAgents: opencode 1.18.35, `google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview` (the default).\nData: all 6,703 included forced-choice pragmods trials. Run outputs that are\nsmall are committed under `data/rsa/loop_smoke/` (report, history, ledger,\neach candidate's files and agent logs; not `.fit_cache/`, `responses.csv`\nor opencode's `.xdg_data/` snapshots).\n\n## Verdict\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/user/auto-psych/docs/auto_rsa/SMOKE_RESULTS.md","text":"# RSA loop smoke test with real Gemini agents: results\n\nRun 2026-10-06 23:14 to 2026-10-07 00:11 UTC (56 min), in a disposable\nclaude.ai cloud container (4 CPUs), on branch `auto-rsa-smoke` (from\n`auto-rsa` at `c1dd78f`), following `HANDOFF_smoke_test.md`:\n\n```bash\nuv run python -m src.rsa.loop.run --results data/rsa/loop_smoke \\\n    --max-iterations 1 --candidate-count 3 --no-sandbox \\\n    --num-warmup 500 --num-samples 500 --num-chains 2\n```\n\nAgents: opencode 1.18.35, `google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview` (the default).\nData: all 6,703 included forced-choice pragmods trials. Run outputs that are\nsmall are committed under `data/rsa/loop_smoke/` (report, history, ledger,\neach candidate's files and agent logs; not `.fit_cache/`, `responses.csv`\nor opencode's `.xdg_data/` snapshots).\n\n## Verdict\n\n**Success on the handoff's criterion. The self-check did not work in this run,\nand the repair path was never exercised.**\n\n- Two of three slots wrote a memo model that passed every gate (code, contract,\n  fit, convergence, finite ELPD, novelty) on the **first** attempt. One of\n  them, `rsa_l2_salience`, is the new best model: −4955.5, which is\n  17.7 ± 2.7 nats ahead of the previous best seed `rsa_l1_salience`.\n- Neither agent ever saw a self-check result. Every self-check call failed: on\n  a CLI bug (now fixed) or on opencode's 120 s shell timeout (fixed in the\n  brief and primer). Their models passed because they stayed close to the\n  seed files' memo, not because the self-check caught anything.\n- One slot (refine incumbent) wrote no `candidate.py`, twice. No candidate was\n  rejected, so **no repair ran**. Whether the rejection reasons let a repair\n  succeed is still untested.\n- The agents hit **no memo errors at all**. Each one read seed files first and\n  changed them in small steps (a new prior, an extra level). The primer's\n  gotchas were not tested in anger.\n\n## Per slot\n\n| Slot | Role | Outcome | Model | ELPD-LOO | Novelty: nearest (RMSE) | Self-check | Agent wall time | Tokens (in / cached / out / reasoning) | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | explore (lens: \"a genuinely different family\") | **admitted** | `literal_speaker_inverter` | −5046.1 (90.6 ± 15.3 behind) | `rsa_l1_shared_prior` (0.045) | 3 calls: usage error, then killed at 120 s twice | 12 min | 77k / 63k / 0.8k / 15.1k | $0.36 |\n| 2 | refine incumbent (`rsa_l1_salience`) | **no file** | (drafts in `/tmp`, never in its dir) | — | — | 1 call, killed at 120 s | 20 min (killed at the limit) | 57k / 152k / 0.9k / 2.0k | $0.18 |\n| 2 retry | same | **no file** | — | — | — | none | 20 min (killed at the limit) | not reported (killed) | ? |\n| 3 | refine chosen | **admitted** | `rsa_l2_salience` | **−4955.5 (best)** | `rsa_l1_salience` (0.010) | 3 calls: usage error, then killed at 120 s twice | 19 min | 39k / 256k / 1.1k / 3.0k | $0.18 |\n\nReported spend: **$0.71** for the round. The killed retry reported no usage,\nso the true figure is a little higher. Token use per slot is small: ~33k of\neach prompt is the brief, primer and handbook, which caching mostly absorbs.\n\nPhase timing: seed fits 6.5 min (5 models); agents 20 min (bound by the\nagent timeout of slot 2); the retry 20 min (killed again); admission fits of\nthe two candidates 4 min; scoring and report 4 min.\n\n### Slot 1: `literal_speaker_inverter` (explore)\n\n> Listeners invert a zero-order \"literal\" speaker (S0) who simply picks one\n> of the object's features uniformly at random, without simulating a\n> listener. The pragmatic preference for simpler objects emerges entirely\n> from probability dilution.\n\nThis is a genuinely different mechanism: L1 over a literal speaker, with no\ninformativity term, and a prior on familiarization, grayscale and feature\ncount. It is 90.6 nats behind the best model, yet clearly better than the\nliteral listener. It is a useful contrast: the data favour a speaker who\nweighs informativity. The agent spent its first step on 15k reasoning tokens\nand wrote all three files in one heredoc, and its memo compiled first time.\n\n### Slot 2: refine incumbent, no candidate (twice)\n\nThe first agent read the seeds and drafted a depth-2 salience model in\n`/tmp/cand_l2/` and in `test_l2_salience.py` **in the repository root**. It ran\nthe self-check on `/tmp/cand_l2`, which was killed at 120 s. It then pivoted to\nan L1 + grayscale-prior variant (`/tmp/cand_l1_color/`, `test_l1_color.py` in\nthe repo root), and was killed at the 1,200 s agent limit while that check\nran. It never wrote to its candidate directory.\n\nThe retry agent's first event came 13 min 46 s after spawn (one `cat` of a\nseed file); it then went silent until the 20 min limit killed it. This looks\nlike Gemini preview latency or a stalled request, not anything in the brief.\n\nUnder bubblewrap (production) the stray files in the repo root would have\nfailed (read-only tree), and so would the `/tmp` drafts once the sandbox was\ngone. Either way the slot would be empty.\n\n### Slot 3: `rsa_l2_salience` (refine chosen)\n\n> I refine the `rsa_l2` model by giving its top-level pragmatic listener the\n> learned salience prior over objects (based on feature count and\n> familiarization) …\n\nThis is a single stated change, the refinement brief's intended use: the\nsalience prior of `rsa_l1_salience` grafted onto the top level of `rsa_l2`.\nIt is the round's discovery. The best model now has a depth-2 listener *and*\na salience prior, and leads by 17.7 nats (6.5 SE). Its novelty margin is small\nbut clear: RMSE 0.010 from `rsa_l1_salience`, against a threshold of 0.002.\nSlot 2's abandoned draft was nearly the same model (the prior at every level),\nso the two refinement slots converged on one idea.\n\n## Bugs found and fixed (each with a test)\n\n1. **The documented self-check command did not parse.**\n   - **Cause:** The brief and primer give\n     `check_candidate <dir> --responses <csv>`, but the tyro CLI declared\n     `candidate_dir` as a required `--candidate-dir` flag.\n   - **Effect:** All three agents ran the documented form first and got a\n     usage error.\n   - **Fix:** `candidate_dir` is now positional\n     (`tyro.conf.Positional`, `src/rsa/loop/check_candidate.py`).\n   - **Test:** `tests/test_rsa_loop_brief.py::test_the_documented_self_check_command_parses`\n     parses the command exactly as `CHECK_COMMAND` writes it.\n2. **The self-check never finished inside an agent.**\n   - **Cause:** opencode's shell tool kills a command after 120 s by default.\n     The self-check takes 2 min 11 s on an idle 4-CPU container, and longer\n     while three agents run checks at once.\n   - **Effect:** All five correctly formed self-check calls were killed\n     before printing a verdict.\n   - **Verified:** opencode's shell tool accepts a `timeout` parameter, and a\n     150 s command with `timeout: 300000` completed.\n   - **Fix:** `CONTEXT.md` (`brief.CHECK_SHELL_TIMEOUT_MS` = 900,000 ms) and the\n     primer's self-check section now say to pass that timeout, to run the\n     check once per change, and to write and test files **only in the\n     candidate directory** (slot 2's failure).\n   - **Test:** asserted in `test_each_role_gets_its_documents_and_the_handbook`.\n3. **No `token_usage.jsonl`.**\n   - **Cause:** The handoff says the launcher writes it, but\n     `src/rsa/loop/run.py` never started the usage log.\n   - **Effect:** This run's spend above is read from the agents' own logs.\n   - **Fix:** The CLI now starts the usage log at\n     `<results>/token_usage.jsonl` and writes `token_usage_summary.json` in a\n     `finally`, as the PyMC loop does.\n   - **Test:** `tests/test_rsa_loop_run.py`.\n\n## Other findings and recommendations\n\n**Recommendations, in priority order:**\n\n1. **Raise `--agent-timeout-sec` from 1,200 to ~2,400 for production.** With\n   a working self-check, a careful agent now runs 1 to 3 checks of 2 to 10 min\n   each. 20 min would end many agents mid-check. Slot 3 already used 19 min,\n   mostly waiting on killed checks.\n2. **Turn off opencode's snapshots for loop agents.** opencode keeps a git\n   snapshot of its working directory in each agent's private `XDG_DATA_HOME`\n   (`<candidate>/.xdg_data/opencode/snapshot`). With the agents running from\n   the repo root, that was ~1 GB per agent: **3.9 GB for this four-agent\n   round**. At 5 rounds × 6 slots plus retries and repairs on Sherlock, that\n   is 30 to 60 GB per run. The scrubbed agent tree will be smaller, but not\n   free. opencode's config has `\"snapshot\": false`. Set it in the launcher's\n   per-agent opencode config (`src/runtime/coding_agent.py`, shared with the\n   PyMC loop, so not changed here), or delete `.xdg_data/opencode/snapshot`\n   when the agent ends.\n3. **Pruning at 2 × clustered dse prunes nothing on pragmods.** The end-of-run\n   prune kept all 7 models, even `literal_listener` at 572 nats behind. The\n   stimulus-clustered SE over the 59 display clusters is large: 331.6 for\n   `literal_listener`, a ratio of 1.73. Every model's ratio to the best was\n   between 1.2 and 1.7. A few large, heterogeneous clusters (displays shared\n   across experiments) dominate the variance. Pruning works as written, but on\n   this data it never fires, so the live set will hit the cap of 8 by round 2\n   and the cap will do all the selection. Consider clustering by display\n   *within* experiment, a lower multiplier, or a per-cluster sign test.\n   Decide before the production run; it is a design choice, not a bug.\n4. **Re-run the smoke test** with the fixes, before Sherlock. It should take\n   ~1 h; consider `--candidate-count 3 --max-iterations 2` so that round 2\n   sees a live set with agent-written models. Look for: (a) agents now\n   reading a PASS/FAIL line; (b) at least one rejection, so the repair path\n   runs. If none comes naturally, a deliberately strict novelty threshold for\n   one round would force rejections.\n5. **Gemini latency.** The retry agent took 13 min 46 s to make its first tool\n   call. If that recurs, try a Flash model for retries (`--agent-model`), or\n   accept the cost of an empty slot.\n6. **The two refinement slots converged** on \"L2 + salience\". With 6 slots\n   (two refining the incumbent) that will happen more. The PyMC loop has the\n   same structure, so watch it in production rather than change it now.\n\n**No new memo gotchas.** No agent hit a memo compile or runtime error, so the\nprimer's list in `rsa_theory.md` and PLAN.md stands unchanged. The agents\nworked around the language by copying the seeds' memo verbatim and changing\n`choice_probs` and the prior. That is safe, but it may keep exploration close\nto the seeds. An exploratory slot that needs new memo structure (a lexical\nuncertainty model, a QUD speaker) is still untested.\n\n**Hypothesis style.** Slot 3's `hypothesis.md` reads as a change log (\"I refine\n`rsa_l2` by …\") rather than a claim a psychologist could test. The refinement\nmenu asks the agent to name the model it refines, so this follows the brief.\nA sentence in the refinement brief could ask for the claim first and the\nlineage second.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":187,"totalLines":187,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/auto_rsa/SMOKE_RESULTS.md","time":{"start":1791346371177,"end":1791346371222}},"id":"prt_114906a5f001fHupP7V7G3nAhN","sessionID":"ses_eeb753877ffeEZ5M2IaWa5Y4aK","messageID":"msg_114905f42001FVTxE0WOSiCNb7"}}
